※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=19 diag=none

※ Where a value is wanted of an `if`, every arm produces one and they are all
※ of one type, and there has to be an `else`: an `if` without one has a way
※ through that runs no arm, and that way would owe a value it has nowhere to
※ get.  It is the same rule `match` follows and the same machinery underneath.

let a: mut u8 = 3u8
let b: mut u8 = 9u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    let first: u8 = if a < b:
        4u8
    elif a = b:
        5u8
    else:
        6u8
    ※ an arm may leave the function instead of producing a value
    let second: u8 = if a > b:
        return 1u8
    else:
        15u8
    first + second
